1. Create a new layout file for the toolbar and define its properties such as background color, title text, etc.

2. In your fragment's XML layout file, add the toolbar element and set its attributes such as id, visibility, etc.

3. In your fragment's Java or Kotlin code, find the toolbar view by its id using findViewById() method.

4. Set the toolbar as the action bar of the activity by calling setSupportActionBar() method on the activity object.

5. Customize the toolbar by setting its properties such as title text, menu items, etc.

Here is an example code snippet to add a toolbar in a fragment:

```
// Step 1: Create a new layout file for the toolbar
<androidx.appcompat.widget.Toolbar
    android:id="@+id/toolbar"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="?attr/actionBarSize"
    android:background="?attr/colorPrimary"
    android:elevation="4dp"
    android:theme="@style/ThemeOverlay.AppCompat.Dark.ActionBar"
    app:popupTheme="@style/ThemeOverlay.AppCompat.Light" />

// Step 2: Add the toolbar element in the fragment's XML layout file
<fragment
    android:id="@+id/my_fragment"
    android:name="com.example.myapp.MyFragment"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent">

    <include layout="@layout/toolbar_layout" />
</fragment>

// Step 3: Find the toolbar view by its id in the fragment's Java or Kotlin code
Toolbar toolbar = getView().findViewById(R.id.toolbar);

// Step 4: Set the toolbar as the action bar of the activity
((AppCompatActivity) getActivity()).setSupportActionBar(toolbar);

// Step 5: Customize the toolbar by setting its properties
toolbar.setTitle("My Fragment");
toolbar.setNavigationIcon(R.drawable.ic_back);
toolbar.setNavigationOnClickListener(new View.OnClickListener() {
    @Override
    public void onClick(View v) {
        // Handle back button click event
    }
});
```

Note that this is just one way to add a toolbar in a fragment. There may be other ways depending on your specific requirements and design.
