Sure! Let's start by creating the `FavoritesManager` class which will handle adding and removing names from the favorites list.

### Step 1: Create the `FavoritesManager` Class

First, let's define the methods for adding and removing favorites. We'll also include logic to save these favorites either in the database (for authenticated users) or in the session (for guests).

```php
// app/Favorites/FavoritesManager.php

namespace App\Favorites;

use App\Models\User;
use App\Models\Favorite;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Session;

class FavoritesManager
{
    public function addFavorite($name)
    {
        if (Auth::check()) {
            // Save favorite to the database
            $favorite = new Favorite();
            $favorite->user_id = Auth::id();
            $favorite->name = $name;
            $favorite->save();
        } else {
            // Save favorite to the session
            $favorites = Session::get('favorites', []);
            $favorites[] = $name;
            Session::put('favorites', array_unique($favorites));
        }
    }

    public function removeFavorite($name)
    {
        if (Auth::check()) {
            // Remove favorite from the database
            Favorite::where('user_id', Auth::id())
                    ->where('name', $name)
                    ->delete();
        } else {
            // Remove favorite from the session
            $favorites = Session::get('favorites', []);
            $favorites = array_diff($favorites, [$name]);
            Session::put('favorites', array_values($favorites));
        }
    }
}
```

### Step 2: Create the `FavoritesController`

Next, let's create the `FavoritesController` which will act as an API endpoint for managing favorites.

```php
// app/Http/Controllers/FavoritesController.php

namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use App\Favorites\FavoritesManager;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Auth;

class FavoritesController extends Controller
{
    protected $favoritesManager;

    public function __construct(FavoritesManager $favoritesManager)
    {
        $this->favoritesManager = $favoritesManager;
    }

    public function add(Request $request)
    {
        $name = $request->input('name');
        $this->favoritesManager->addFavorite($name);

        return response()->json(['message' => 'Favorite added successfully']);
    }

    public function remove(Request $request)
    {
        $name = $request->input('name');
        $this->favoritesManager->removeFavorite($name);

        return response()->json(['message' => 'Favorite removed successfully']);
    }
}
```

### Step 3: Define Routes

Finally, let's define the routes in your `routes/api.php` file to map the endpoints to the controller actions.

```php
// routes/api.php

use App\Http\Controllers\FavoritesController;

Route::post('/favorites/add', [FavoritesController::class, 'add']);
Route::post('/favorites/remove', [FavoritesController::class, 'remove']);
```

With these steps, you now have a basic setup for handling favorites in both authenticated and unauthenticated states using Laravel, Inertia, and Vue.js. You can expand upon this foundation as needed for your application.

