To manage the maximum number of running coroutines in PHP, you can use a combination of semaphores and queues to control the concurrency.

Here's an example implementation:

```php
class CoroutineManager {
    private $semaphore;
    private $queue;

    public function __construct($maxCoroutines) {
        $this->semaphore = new Semaphore($maxCoroutines);
        $this->queue = new Queue();
    }

    public function addJob(callable $job) {
        $this->queue->push($job);
    }

    public function run() {
        while (!$this->queue->isEmpty()) {
            if ($this->semaphore->acquire()) {
                $job = $this->queue->pop();
                go(function () use ($job) {
                    try {
                        $job();
                    } finally {
                        $this->semaphore->release();
                    }
                });
            }
        }
    }
}

// Usage example:
$manager = new CoroutineManager(5);

for ($i = 0; $i < 10; $i++) {
    $manager->addJob(function () {
        // Your job code here
    });
}

$manager->run();
```

In this example, we create a `CoroutineManager` class that takes the maximum number of coroutines as a parameter. The `addJob` method adds a job to the queue, and the `run` method starts executing the jobs concurrently using the semaphore to limit the number of running coroutines.

Note: This example assumes you have the necessary extensions installed (e.g., `swoole`) for coroutine support in PHP.
